  pages={1-6},
  abstract={Medical imaging methods, including CT (Computerised Tomography), MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging), PET (Positron Emission Tomography), and ultrasound, provide different perspectives on the same physiological function or anatomical structure. Biomedical image fusion creates a single composite image by merging complementary data from multiple imaging modalities for increasing diagnosis accuracy. A parameter-adaptive Pulse-Coupled Neural Network (PA-PCNN) architecture was proposed in this study for multimodal fusion in the Non-Subsampled Contourlet Transform (NSCT) domain. NSCT separates source images into bands of high and low frequencies, while PA-PCNN optimizes fusion rules in an adaptive manner.The novelty of this work lies in the dynamic coefficient-selection strategy that jointly exploits NSCT’s shift-invariant directional representation and PA-PCNN’s adaptive firing mechanism, enabling superior preservation of fine anatomical details and edge structures compared with existing techniques.This proposed method, assessed on PET-SPECT and CT-MRI datasets, demonstrates superior performance compared to existing techniques, achieving a PSNR of 34.6 dB, an SSIM of 0.96, and a mutual information (MI) score of 4.12.},
  keywords={Ultrasonic imaging;Magnetic resonance imaging;Image edge detection;Computed tomography;Transforms;Transformers;Robustness;Discrete wavelet transforms;Positron emission tomography;Image fusion;Medical image fusion;NSCT;PA-PCNN;multimodal imaging;and brain image enhancement},
